## **13A. Introduction** {.unlisted}

The spotted lanternfly (SLF), *Lycorma delicatula* (White) (Hemiptera: Fulgoridae), is a highly polyphagous planthopper that feeds on a broad range of agricultural and non-agricultural plants, including grapevines, fruit and nut trees, and ornamental species (`\hyperref[ref-chu-1930]{Chu 1930}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-chu-1930">Chu 1930</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-barringer-ciafre-2020]{Barringer and Ciafré 2020}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-barringer-ciafre-2020">Barringer and Ciafré 2020</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-eppo-2025]{EPPO 2025}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-eppo-2025">EPPO 2025</a>`{=html}). Adults cause direct damage by feeding on plant sap, while the accumulation of honeydew promotes the growth of sooty mold, which can inhibit photosynthesis and attract additional pests (`\hyperref[ref-dara-2015]{Dara et al. 2015}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-dara-2015">Dara et al. 2015</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-urban-leach-2023]{Urban and Leach 2023}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-urban-leach-2023">Urban and Leach 2023</a>`{=html}). Native to China, SLF was first detected in the United States in 2014 in Pennsylvania and has since spread to at least 20 eastern states (`\hyperref[ref-urban-leach-2023]{Urban and Leach 2023}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-urban-leach-2023">Urban and Leach 2023</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-nysipm-2025]{NYSIPM 2025}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-nysipm-2025">NYSIPM 2025</a>`{=html}). The widespread presence of its preferred host, tree-of-heaven [*Ailanthus altissima* (Mill.) Swingle], has facilitated its rapid expansion (`\hyperref[ref-barringer-ciafre-2020]{Barringer and Ciafré 2020}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-barringer-ciafre-2020">Barringer and Ciafré 2020</a>`{=html}). In suburban and urban environments, SLF can become a significant nuisance, with large aggregations of adults often swarming on trees (`\hyperref[ref-urban-leach-2023]{Urban and Leach 2023}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-urban-leach-2023">Urban and Leach 2023</a>`{=html}). The species has already caused substantial economic impacts, including estimated annual losses of $8.8 million to New York’s grape industry alone (`\hyperref[ref-pinto-2025]{Pinto et al. 2025}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-pinto-2025">Pinto et al. 2025</a>`{=html}).

Modeling studies indicate that SLF has not yet filled its fundamental niche in the contiguous United States (CONUS) under current weather patterns, with suitable conditions extending across much of the Pacific Coast and eastern United States (`\hyperref[ref-jones-2022]{Jones et al. 2022}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-jones-2022">Jones et al. 2022</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-wakie-2019]{Wakie et al. 2019}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-wakie-2019">Wakie et al. 2019</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-barker-2025]{Barker et al. 2025}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-barker-2025">Barker et al. 2025</a>`{=html}). The coldest regions of the CONUS, particularly portions of the Midwest and Northeast, may be less suitable for establishment (`\hyperref[ref-wakie-2019]{Wakie et al. 2019}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-wakie-2019">Wakie et al. 2019</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-barker-2025]{Barker et al. 2025}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-barker-2025">Barker et al. 2025</a>`{=html}), although urban heat island effects could facilitate establishment in colder areas beyond the currently predicted range (`\hyperref[ref-owen-2024]{Owen et al. 2024}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-owen-2024">Owen et al. 2024</a>`{=html}). SLF phenology may also shift in response to changing weather patterns. Analysis of citizen science observations from 2014 to 2022 found earlier adult emergence and later winter die-off as SLF expanded its range in the eastern United States (`\hyperref[ref-owen-2024]{Owen et al. 2024}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-owen-2024">Owen et al. 2024</a>`{=html}). However, the effects of temperature, photoperiod, and other environmental factors on egg diapause and developmental rates remain incompletely understood, limiting predictions of how SLF may respond to changing weather patterns (`\hyperref[ref-keena-nielsen-2021]{Keena and Nielsen 2021}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-keena-nielsen-2021">Keena and Nielsen 2021</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-calvin-2023]{Calvin et al. 2023}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-calvin-2023">Calvin et al. 2023</a>`{=html}). Diapause may buffer populations against weather variability by enhancing overwintering egg survival and accelerating development and fecundity during summer and fall heat waves (`\hyperref[ref-lewkiewicz-2024]{Lewkiewicz et al. 2024}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-lewkiewicz-2024">Lewkiewicz et al. 2024</a>`{=html}). SLF is currently considered univoltine, including in warmer regions of China, and whether it can complete multiple generations annually in warmer areas of the United States remains uncertain (`\hyperref[ref-keena-nielsen-2021]{Keena and Nielsen 2021}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-keena-nielsen-2021">Keena and Nielsen 2021</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-calvin-2023]{Calvin et al. 2023}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-calvin-2023">Calvin et al. 2023</a>`{=html}).

A model for spotted lanternfly developed for the Degree-Day, establishment Risk, and Phenological event mapping system (DDRP) is operationalized to produce near real-time forecasts for the CONUS at <https://uspest.org/DDRP>. The phenology model applies a lower developmental threshold of 10 °C and upper threshold of 35 °C for all life stages (egg, larva, pupa, and adult) and assumes the egg is the overwintering stage. The climatic suitability model applies a lower cold stress threshold and upper heat stress threshold of −16 °C and 37 °C, respectively. The full set of model parameters for spotted lanternfly, including degree-day requirements for individual life stages and climate stress limits, are presented in @tbl-slf-parameters. Details on model parameterization are available at USPest.org (`\hyperref[ref-barker-2025]{Barker et al. 2025}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-barker-2025">Barker et al. 2025</a>`{=html}). 

## **13B. Results** {.unlisted}

### **i. Phenology**

#### **Entire period: 1981--2025**

Over 1981–2025, predicted first egg hatch and adult emergence showed mixed spatial trends, with later events occurring across portions of the northern and central United States and earlier events in scattered southern and western areas (@fig-slf-emergence, @fig-slf-egg-hatch). Significant changes were concentrated in the eastern and central United States and in localized western areas.


